WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.740
The first film showed tissues switching T4 on, to T3.

2
00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:07.560
But T4 is built in the gland, from iodine.

3
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.740
Here is a thyroid scan, glowing.

4
00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:14.920
Does a bright gland mean plenty of hormone in the blood?

5
00:00:18.383 --> 00:00:23.823
Zoom into one follicle: a hollow ball of cells around a protein gel, the colloid.

6
00:00:25.123 --> 00:00:28.423
Each cell faces blood on one side and colloid on the other.

7
00:00:29.723 --> 00:00:34.263
On the blood side, the sodium-iodide symporter, NIS, pulls iodide in.

8
00:00:35.233 --> 00:00:41.133
At the colloid face, the enzyme thyroperoxidase fixes iodine onto a large protein,

9
00:00:41.633 --> 00:00:47.953
thyroglobulin. Iodinated pieces join into T4 and T3, still on the protein.

10
00:00:49.313 --> 00:00:52.913
Driven by TSH, the cell later takes colloid back in.

11
00:00:54.053 --> 00:00:58.073
Mostly T4 leaves, through transporters such as MCT8.

12
00:00:59.067 --> 00:01:00.987
A scan borrows the first step.

13
00:01:02.207 --> 00:01:07.527
A small tracer dose of radioactive iodine is given, commonly iodine-123.

14
00:01:09.127 --> 00:01:11.747
NIS carries it in along with ordinary iodide.

15
00:01:13.327 --> 00:01:17.207
Hours later, a camera measures what fraction the gland holds, and where.

16
00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:19.790
Here is the one idea.

17
00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:24.010
Uptake reports how much tracer the gland is still holding.

18
00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:30.350
That depends on trapping, on fixing iodine to protein, and on the iodide supply.

19
00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:33.710
It does not measure the hormone in the blood.

20
00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:37.090
The tracer never travels alone.

21
00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:42.470
Ordinary iodide from the diet rides in beside it, through the same doors.

22
00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:47.790
Now suppose a large iodine load arrives, such as contrast dye.

23
00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:50.490
Does uptake rise or fall?

24
00:01:56.467 --> 00:02:01.307
It falls, partly because the flood of ordinary iodide dilutes the tracer.

25
00:02:02.787 --> 00:02:04.727
Now flip it: a diet short of iodine.

26
00:02:06.067 --> 00:02:09.607
Little ordinary iodide competes, so uptake runs high.

27
00:02:10.927 --> 00:02:12.407
Yet blood T4 can be falling.

28
00:02:13.517 --> 00:02:15.217
The scan also shows where.

29
00:02:16.517 --> 00:02:19.997
In a toxic adenoma, one nodule makes hormone on its own.

30
00:02:21.237 --> 00:02:24.417
The extra hormone suppresses the pituitary's TSH.

31
00:02:25.917 --> 00:02:29.337
Without that drive, the rest of the gland takes up little tracer.

32
00:02:30.417 --> 00:02:33.617
So the scan is read with the blood tests, never alone.

33
00:02:34.917 --> 00:02:37.077
The blood tests report the hormone level.

34
00:02:38.277 --> 00:02:41.817
The scan reports what the gland is doing with iodide, and where.

35
00:02:42.867 --> 00:02:46.247
A bright gland is a busy trap, not a full bloodstream.

36
00:02:47.607 --> 00:02:51.407
The scan pictures tracer held in the gland, not hormone in the blood.
